897:
(a) The following acts or omissions are declared to be violations of this title:
(1) Engaging in any business, profession, trade or calling without procuring the proper business tax registration to do so as provided in this title, if required, or without registering with the city as required in section 30-1-7;
(2) Failing to make the return or statement to the director of community and economic development as required by section 30-1-9(b);
(3) Giving or filing, or directing the giving or filing, of any false information regarding any tax due under the provisions of this title or upon which tax due under this title is based or determined;
(4) Failing or refusing to permit the director of community and economic development to examine books or records as provided in section 30-1-10;
(5) Failure to comply with any federal, state or city law or regulation applicable to the particular business being conducted; or
(6) Failure to comply with any of the requirements of this title.
(b) Any person who shall fail to pay the occupation tax or administrative fee assessed pursuant to this chapter (by the 1 st day of April) shall, upon conviction therefor in the municipal court, be subjected to a civil fine of up to five hundred dollars ($500.00).
(Ord. No. 95-46, § 1, 12-21-95; Ord. No. 13-10, § 2, 8-13-13)

899:
(a) The occupation tax or other fee imposed in this chapter shall not apply to any business, profession, trade or calling involved solely in interstate or foreign commerce, unless and until the same shall cease to be a part of such interstate or foreign commerce.
(b) The legal right of the producer who sells the product of his own farm is recognized, but this right does not authorize any person purchasing goods or other things of value within or without the city to offer the same for sale within the city without proper business tax registration.
(c) Before any disabled veteran or blind person shall be granted a permit to enter business without the payment of an occupation fee or tax in the city, such disabled veteran or blind person shall present to the director of economic and community development a certificate of exemption from the payment of such fee issued by the State Commissioner of Veterans' Service.
(d) Any practitioner whose office is maintained by and who is employed in practice exclusively by the United States, the state, a municipality or county of the state, instrumentalities of the state, or a municipality or county of the state shall not be required to pay an occupation tax or administrative fee for that practice.
(e) The occupation tax or other fee in this chapter shall not apply to nonprofit organizations.
(f) The following businesses are not covered by the provisions of this chapter but may be assessed an occupation tax, administrative fee or other type of tax pursuant to the provisions of other general laws of the State of Georgia or by local law:
(1) Those businesses regulated by the Georgia Public Service Commission;
(2) Those electrical service businesses organized under Chapter 3 of Title 46 of the Official Code of Georgia Annotated;
(3) Any farm operation for the production from or on the land of agricultural products, but not including agribusiness;
(4) Cooperative marketing associations governed by O.C.G.A. Section 2-10-105;
(5) Insurance companies governed by O.C.G.A. Section 33-8-8, et seq.;
(6) Motor common carriers governed by O.C.G.A. Section 46-7-15;
(7) Those businesses governed by O.C.G.A. Section 48-5-355; (Businesses that purchase carload lots of guano, meats, meal, flour, bran, cottonseed, or cottonseed meal and hulls.)
(8) Agricultural products and livestock raised in the State of Georgia, governed by O.C.G.A. Section 48-5-356;
(9) Depository financial institutions governed by O.C.G.A. Section 48-6-93; and
(10) Facilities operated by a charitable trust governed by O.C.G.A. Section 48-13-55.
(g) The administrative fee and occupation tax upon real estate brokers shall be in accordance with O.C.G.A. Section 48-13-17 and the fee schedule set forth in this chapter.
(Ord. No. 95-46, § 1, 12-21-95)

981:
Any person desiring to secure a permit under this division shall apply therefor in writing over his signature to the chief of police on forms provided by the city, and such application shall state the:
(1) Name and address of the applicant;
(2) Name and address of the person by whom employed;
(3) Length of service of such applicant with such employer;
(4) Place of residence and nature of the employment of the applicant during the preceding twelve (12) months;
(5) Nature or character of the goods, wares, merchandise or services to be offered by the applicant; and
(6) Personal description of the applicant.
Such application shall be accompanied by such credentials and other evidence of the good moral character and identity of the applicant as may be reasonably required by the chief of police.
(Code 1972, § 15-117)

995:
Applicants for license required by this article shall file a written application signed by the applicant with the assistant city manager, which application shall contain and show:
(1) The name or names of the person or persons having management or supervision of the applicant's business during the time the same is proposed to be conducted or carried on in the city; the local address of such person or persons so engaged in the city; the permanent address of such person or persons; the capacity in which such person or persons will act (that is, whether as proprietor, agent or otherwise); and if a corporation, the state in which the same is incorporated, whether the corporation is domesticated or licensed to do business in the State of Georgia, the registered agent of the corporation in the State of Georgia and the designated agent for the service of process in the State of Georgia;
(2) The place or places in the city where the applicant proposes to carry on the business described in its application; the hours in which the applicant proposes to keep said business open to the public; and the length of time during which the applicant proposes to conduct any such business;
(3) The place or places, other than the permanent place of business of the applicant, where the applicant within the six-month period next preceding the date of such application conducted a transient business as described in this article, stating the nature of such business and giving the post office and street address of the location in which such business was conducted;
(4) A statement as to the nature, character and quality of the goods, wares and merchandise to be sold; whether the same are proposed to be sold from stock in possession or by sample; and whether the same are to be sold by direct sale or by taking orders for future delivery only;
(5) Credentials or other necessary written authorization from the person for whom the applicant proposes to do business, authorizing the applicant to act for and on behalf of such person as his representative; and
(6) Such other reasonable information pertaining to the identity or character of the person or persons having the management or supervision of the applicant's business or the method or plan of doing such business that the assistant city manager or his agent may deem necessary or proper to fulfill the purpose of this article in the protection of the public good.
(Code 1972, § 15-174; Ord. No. 07-12, § 7, 4-24-07; Ord. No. 16-12, § 8, 12-13-16)
State Law reference— Foreign corporations, O.C.G.A. § 14-2-310 et seq.

1000:
(a) The license issued pursuant to this article may be revoked by the mayor and council after reasonable notice to the licensee and an opportunity for a hearing thereon for any one (1) or more of the following reasons:
(1) Fraud, misrepresentation or false statements contained in the application for license;
(2) Fraud, misrepresentation or false statements made in conjunction with the sale of goods, wares or merchandise;
(3) Violation of any of the provisions of this article;
(4) Conviction of the licensee of any felony, or of any misdemeanor or crime involving moral turpitude; or
(5) Conducting the business licensed pursuant to the provisions of this article in an unlawful manner, in a manner to constitute a public nuisance or in a manner to constitute a breach of peace or to constitute a menace to health, safety or the general welfare of the public.
(b) Notice of hearing for revocation of a license shall be given in writing to the licensee, setting forth specifically the grounds of the complaint and the time, date and place of the hearing thereon. Such notice shall be served on the licensee by handing the same to the person in charge of the licensed business or by mailing the same postage prepaid to the licensee at his last known address or to the designated agent for the service of process or the registered agent of the licensee, at least five (5) days prior to the date set for the hearing.
(c) Service of the notice shall suspend the license pending the outcome of the hearing, and any business operation conducted pursuant to the provisions of the license shall promptly cease and terminate.
(Code 1972, § 15-180)

1019:
The provisions of this article shall not apply to or affect:
(1) Persons selling goods or personal property at a:
a. Lien foreclosure sale authorized and conducted pursuant to law;
b. Judicial sale pursuant to the order of any court of competent jurisdiction; or
c. Sale by a trustee, executor, administrator or other fiduciary authorized or required by law;
(2) Any public officials acting in accordance with and pursuant to their duties, powers and responsibilities as public officials;
(3) Any sale conducted by any merchant or mercantile or other business establishment from or at a place of business, provided such sale is licensed as required by law and permitted by the zoning regulations of the city either as a conforming or an existing nonconforming use under the provisions thereof or any other sale conducted by a manufacturer, dealer or vendor otherwise authorized by law and which is conducted from properly zoned premises and which is not otherwise prohibited by the ordinances of the city; or
(4) A sale by an exempt organization as defined in this article, provided such sale is conducted on not more than two (2) occasions of not more than three (3) consecutive days in any calendar year.
(Code 1972, § 15-160)
State Law reference— Executions and judicial sales, O.C.G.A. § 9-13-1 et seq.; sales of abandoned property by state revenue commissioner, O.C.G.A. § 44-12-209 et seq.; sale of property for municipal taxes, O.C.G.A. § 48-5-359; sales by administrators or executors, O.C.G.A. § 53-8-20 et seq.

1095:
(a) Reasons for denial of a special event permit include, but are not limited to:
(1) The special event will unnecessarily disrupt traffic within the city beyond practical solution;
(2) The special event will interfere with access to fire stations and fire hydrants;
(3) The location of the special event will cause undue hardship to adjacent businesses or residents;
(4) The special event will cause unnecessary disruption of public services which would unreasonably impact the remainder of the city;
(5) The application contains incomplete or false information; and
(6) The producer fails to comply with any terms required by this article.
(b) Reasons for revocation of a special events permit include, but are not limited to:
(1) False or incomplete information within the application;
(2) Failure to comply with all terms and conditions of the permit;
(3) Failure to arrange for or adequately remit all fees, deposits, insurance or bonds to the city; and
(4) Existence of disaster, public calamity, riot or other emergency as the city determines, in its sole discretion, to be an impact upon the public health, safety and welfare.
(c) Further, a special event permit may be denied, suspended, or revoked by the city manager, if the city manager determines that the health, welfare, or safety of the public may be endangered.
(Ord. No. 14-11, § 4, 10-14-14)

1116:
It shall be unlawful for any person to operate a taxicab in the city unless such taxicab shall be equipped as follows:
(1) The name of the entity, enterprise or company shall be painted or affixed by decal to and shall appear on the left and right front doors of all vehicles operated as taxicabs;
(2) A dome light shall be permanently affixed to the top of any vehicle operated as a taxicab, bearing the term "taxi" or the name of the entity, business or company operating the taxi business. The sign shall be lighted at all times while the headlights of such vehicle are required to be displayed pursuant to O.C.G.A. § 40-8-20;
(3) Each vehicle operated as a taxicab by licensee shall either have painted or affixed by decal an identifying number, unique to each vehicle, on the left and right rear quarter panel and on the right-hand side of the most vertical surface of the rear trunk.
(Code 1972, § 25-33; Ord. No. 96-62, § 4, 12-19-96)

1147:
An application for a franchise under this article shall be made with the assistant city manager by petition, which shall set forth the name, address and place of business of the applicant, if an individual, and if a partnership the name and address and place of business of each partner, and the place of business, and if a corporation, the name and address of each of the officers of the corporation, and the place of business of such corporation; the trade name under which the applicant does or proposes to do business; the name of the insurance company with which the applicant has made arrangements to secure insurance as required by the provisions set forth herein; together with the following information, to wit:
(1) Written assurance and proof that all personnel of applicant who will provide ambulance services are certified emergency medical technicians, cardiac technicians or paramedics as those positions are certified by the State of Georgia Department of Human Resources;
(2) Written assurance and proof that each ambulance to be used by applicant within the city is certified by the State of Georgia Department of Human Resources;
(3) Written assurance and proof that applicant will maintain within the city limits a minimum of two (2) ambulances, neither of which shall exceed sixty (60) months in age (remounted vehicles will be deemed new as of the date the chassis is originally titled), equipped and staffed to provide advanced life support services, as defined by the State of Georgia Department of Human Resources, at all times except when such ambulance(s) shall be outside of the city limits while responding to or on a call for ambulances services, in addition to written assurance and proof that each ambulance maintained hereunder shall be staffed by at least one (1) paramedic;
(4) Written assurance and proof that applicant is properly licensed by the State of Georgia to provide ambulance services within the state;
(5) Written assurance and proof that the applicant will either install mobile or make available portable radio units for each ambulance operated within the city which are capable of communications with city police and fire department personnel;
(6) Written assurance and proof that applicant will maintain a written log of requests for service calls and will in addition make such written records available for inspection by the city upon request all in order to determine compliance with the requirements hereunder;
(7) Written assurance and proof that all requirements of this article and all other applicable ordinances of the city and statutes of the State of Georgia have been met and fully complied with in order to engage in ambulance services;
(8) Written assurance of state mandated workers' compensation insurance and proof of public liability insurance in an amount not less than 1.5 million dollars ($1,500,000.00) for one injury, not less than 1.5 million dollars ($1,500,000.00) for all injuries out of one (1) accident, not less than 1.5 million dollars ($1,500,000.00) property damage, and not less than 1.5 million dollars ($1,500,000.00) for injuries caused by malpractice;
Together with the written application described herein, applicant shall submit to the assistant city manager by certified check a three hundred dollar ($300.00) processing fee to cover expenses of investigation and processing of the application, which fee shall not be refundable.
(Ord. No. 96-55, § 1, 10-22-96; Ord. No. 07-12, § 7, 4-24-07; Ord. No. 16-12, § 8, 12-13-16)

1149:
(a) The franchise issued pursuant to this article may be revoked by the mayor and council after reasonable notice to the licensee and an opportunity for a hearing thereon for any one (1) or more of the following reasons:
(1) Fraud, misrepresentation or false statements contained in the petition for franchise;
(2) Fraud, misrepresentation or false statements made in conjunction with the provision of ambulance services hereunder;
(3) Violation of any of the provisions of this article;
(4) Conducting the business franchise pursuant to the provisions of this article in an unlawful manner, in a manner to constitute a public nuisance, or in manner to constitute a breach of peace or constitute a menace to the health, safety or the general welfare of the public.
(b) Notice of hearing for revocation of a franchise shall be given in writing to the franchisee, setting forth specifically the grounds of the complaint and the time, date and place of the hearing thereon. Such notice shall be served on the franchisee by handing the same to the person in charge of the franchised business or by mailing the same, postage prepaid to the franchisee at his last known address or to the designated agent for the service of process or the registered agent of the franchisee, at least five (5) days prior to the date set forth the hearing.
(c) Such service of the notice shall suspend the license pending the outcome of the hearing, and any business operation conducted pursuant to the provisions of the franchise shall promptly cease and terminate.
(Ord. No. 96-55, § 1, 10-22-96)

1165:
Except as provided in section 3-3-23 of the Official Code of Georgia Annotated or in section 3-20-6 of this Code, as either may now or hereafter exist or be amended:
(1) The furnishing, delivery, or sale of alcoholic beverages, including but not limited to spirituous liquors, malt beverages, beer or wine to person under twenty-one (21) years of age;
(2) The purchase or possession thereof by persons under twenty-one (21) years of age;
(3) The use of false or fraudulent identification by persons under twenty one (21) years of age for the purpose of purchasing, obtaining, or securing the delivery of any such alcoholic beverages; or
(4) The furnishing, delivery, sale or dispensing of any such alcoholic beverages by persons under twenty-one (21) years of age is prohibited.
(Code 1972, § 4-7; Ord. No. 88-4, § 1, 2-23-88; Ord. No. 90-21, § 1, 5-22-90; Ord. No. 00-1, § 1, 2-8-00)

1167:
(a) No person, firm or corporation having a license to sell alcoholic beverages in the city shall allow, permit or require a person in its employment and who is under eighteen (18) years of age to dispense, serve, sell, or take orders for alcoholic beverages;
(b) This section shall not prohibit persons under eighteen (18) years of age who are employed in supermarkets, convenience stores, breweries or drug stores from selling or handling alcoholic beverages which are sold solely for consumption off the premises;
(c) It is the intent of this section that bus-boys cleaning tables that may include or have alcoholic beverages thereon, or cashiers accepting payment for orders which may include alcoholic beverages, or hostesses or kitchen employees who may also perform bus-boy or cashier functions will not be deemed to be involved in the dispensing, serving, selling, handling or taking orders for alcoholic beverages unless it can be proven that any such employee is otherwise involved in the dispensing, serving, selling, handling or taking orders for alcoholic beverages prohibited by (a) of this section.
(Code 1972, § 4-8; Ord. No. 90-21, § 2, 5-22-90; Ord. No. 92-42, § 1, 12-8-92)

1172:
(a) All applications for licenses to sell alcoholic beverages of any kind shall be made in person to the assistant city manager of the city or his designated representative in writing on forms furnished by the city; the application must be completed and signed and shall contain all information reasonably required by the assistant city manager or his designee and shall include but is not necessarily limited to the following:
(1) The name, date of birth, current home address and the length of residency of an applicant in the City of LaGrange, in Troup County, and in the State of Georgia;
(2) The name, location and description of the premises or place of business to be operated under such license;
(3) A statement as to whether the applicant or any person having an interest in the application has made, at any previous time, an application for alcoholic beverage license and if so the disposition of such application;
(4) Whether the applicant or any person having an interest in the application, including the record owner of the premises for which licensing is requested, has ever been convicted of a crime, including traffic violations involving driving under the influence of alcoholic beverages or driving under the influence of a Georgia controlled substance, but not otherwise including traffic offenses. The term "conviction" shall include an adjudication of guilt, first offender status, plea of nolo contendere or the forfeiture of a bond by a person charged with a crime;
(5) Whether a previous license issued to the applicant or any person with an interest in the application has been revoked by the federal government, State of Georgia or any subdivision thereof;
(6) Whether any other person, firm or corporation other than a corporation whose stock is publicly sold and listed on a national stock exchange regulated by the federal securities and exchange commission, has any interest directly or indirectly in the profits or losses of the business;
(7) A statement that the premises for which the license is sought complies with all applicable zoning and building codes and regulations of the City of LaGrange, but the application shall include to the extent necessary plans and specifications for any new construction, additions to or renovations of the premises for which the license is sought;
(8) The application will include information sufficient to show the ownership of the building and shall contain a copy of the lease to the building if the applicant is leasing the building;
(9) The payment at the time of application of any and all application fees required by the applicable provisions of the City Code;
(10) To the extent required by the appropriate provisions of this chapter, a completed statement of the applicant's financial condition sufficient to show financial responsibility of the applicant;
(11) Each applicant shall submit a survey by a registered land surveyor showing compliances with all distance requirements contained herein;
(12) And any and other documentation, information and forms required by other appropriate provisions of this chapter.
(b) Each application, after filing but prior to consideration by the city manager, shall be advertised and public notice thereof given as required by the appropriate provisions of this chapter.
(c) The making of any untrue or misleading statements in the application for license shall, among other reasons, be sufficient cause for refusal, suspension, revocation or cancellation of a license as the city manager or mayor and council may in their discretion deem appropriate.
(Ord. No. 92-32, § 1, 9-22-92; Ord. No. 02-38, § 3, 12-10-02; Ord. No. 07-12, § 7, 4-24-07; Ord. No. 09-14, § 2, 8-25-09; Ord. No. 14-05, § 1, 3-25-14; Ord. No. 16-12, § 8, 12-13-16; Ord. No. 21-17, § 2, 11-9-21)
